7. Identity & Authentication

Administrators configure identity and authentication policies.

Configuration areas

Authentication methods
	•	SSO enable / disable
	•	MFA requirement
	•	national identity authentication

Password policy
	•	password complexity
	•	password expiry
	•	password reuse restrictions

Session security
	•	session timeout
	•	concurrent sessions
	•	login retry limits

Account recovery
	•	password reset configuration
	•	identity verification rules

8. Users & Roles

Administrators manage governance roles and permissions.

Role model

Predefined roles include:
	•	Governance Professional
	•	Director
	•	Observer
	•	System Administrator

Role customization

Administrators can:
	•	modify permissions
	•	clone roles
	•	create custom governance roles

Forum-level permissions

Permissions may vary per governance forum:
	•	board
	•	committee
	•	working group

9. User Lifecycle Management

Administrators manage user lifecycle.

User creation

Users can be created via:
	•	CS onboarding
	•	admin self-service
	•	bulk import

Lifecycle states
	•	active
	•	suspended
	•	offboarded

Term expiry

User roles may automatically expire after a defined date.

Partial role removal

Users can be removed from one forum while remaining active in others.

10. Multi-Organisation Access

Users may belong to multiple organisations.

Shared identity

Users may have a shared identity across multiple organisations.

Organisation switching

Users choose which organisation context they access.

Organisation security policies

Each organisation may define its own:
	•	authentication rules
	•	password policy
	•	access controls

11. Governance Content Protection

Administrators define how governance materials are protected.

Document handling restrictions
	•	disable document export
	•	disable printing
	•	disable copy/paste

Annotation protection

Prevent export of annotations.

Sensitive governance content

Additional protection may apply to:
	•	confidential board materials
	•	AI-generated governance insights

12. Device & Application Security

Administrators define device behaviour and application security.

Device security policies
	•	session timeout
	•	biometric unlock
	•	offline access limits

Application data protection
	•	restrict offline storage
	•	purge cached data
	•	control sync behaviour

Meeting security

Controls such as:
	•	screen lock during meetings
	•	inactivity lock behaviour

Device registration

Administrators may:
	•	register trusted devices
	•	restrict access to approved devices

13. Audit & Compliance

Administrators configure audit logging.

Audit logging

Track events such as:
	•	authentication events
	•	permission changes
	•	configuration changes
	•	document access

Log access

Log access is role-based.

Log retention

Administrators define log retention periods.

Selective logging

Logging may be disabled for specific users.

